nproven probability hypothesis.~
~
The hypothesis comes from the consideration of the following problem:~~
  Suppose that an observer perceives an organization that distributes~~
 a fixed amount of grant money among members of an applicant pool eac~~
h year.  The observer perceives, or can estimate, which applicants re~~
ceive grants and which do not.  The observer can also estimate the di~~
stribution of all applicants over a function(al) of applicant attribu~~
tes which is relevant to grant receipt.~
~
Suppose that the observer wishes to gague the impact of introducing a~~
 single additional applicant to the pool.  Naturally, the applicant's~~
 chance of receiving a grant can be estimated by applying the functio~~
n(al).  However, it is desired to know the total effect on the pool, ~~
i.e. the effect on rejected applicants as well;  if the applicant in ~~
question is accepted, it will result in the rejection of the current ~~
borderline applicant, the applicant "closest" to being rejected befor~~
e acceptance of the applicant under consideration.~
~
The hypothesis is that the distribution of the rejected applicant ove~~
r the function(al) will, in any quantile, be equal to the product of ~~
the percentage of that quantile's population accepted, the percentage~~
 rejected, and the percentage of overall population in that quantile,~~
 divided by the product of the acceptance rate and the rejection rate~~
.~
~
This is tested by assuming a primary metric by which applicants are a~~
ccepted or rejected, of which the observational metric is a normal ap~~
proximation.~
~
The results seem to indicate a very good estimation.Author: Wynship HillierDate: Thu, Jun 28, 2007 9:21 PMSaveauthor: Wynship HillierSavedate: Sat, Jun 30, 2007 4:08 PMDefaultsize: 48,24Diagstate: 1,22,22,712,476,17Fontstyle: Arial, 18Fileinfo: 0,Model Untitled,2,2,0,0,C:\Documents and Settings\Wynship H~~
illier\My Documents\CounterPULSE\continuous test.ANAChance SourceTitle: sourceDescription: This is the distribution of the applicant pool over the p~~
